.PP
By default \fBfree\fR prints the following columns:
.TP
.B TOTAL
Total installed memory (\fBMemTotal\fR in /proc/meminfo). For the swap
row, the size of the swap area (\fBSwapTotal\fR).
.TP
.B USED
Memory in use, computed as \fITOTAL\fR \- \fIAVAILABLE\fR, matching
procps-ng. If the kernel does not provide \fBMemAvailable\fR, the
fallback is \fITOTAL\fR \- \fIFREE\fR \- \fIBUFFERS\fR \- \fICACHE\fR.
.TP
.B FREE
Memory that is not allocated to anything at all (\fBMemFree\fR). On a
healthy busy system this number is typically small — the kernel
prefers to use memory for cache rather than leave it idle.
.TP
.B SHARED
Memory used by tmpfs and shared anonymous mappings (\fBShmem\fR).
.TP
.B BUFF/CACHE
Sum of kernel buffers, page cache, and slab reclaimable memory
(\fBBuffers\fR + \fBCached\fR + \fBSReclaimable\fR). With \fB\-w\fR
this is split into separate \fIBUFFERS\fR and \fICACHE\fR columns.
.TP
.B AVAILABLE
Estimate of how much memory is available for starting new applications
without swapping (\fBMemAvailable\fR, computed by the kernel). This is
usually the field worth looking at — it accounts for reclaimable page
cache and slab memory in addition to truly free pages.
.PP
The swap row uses the corresponding \fBSwap\fR fields and omits
\fISHARED\fR, \fIBUFF/CACHE\fR, and \fIAVAILABLE\fR.
